{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nEnforced `verify-identity --all` (`REBAR_IDENTITY_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATED=1`, no `enforce_since`) exits 1 on the tickets history with 7,007 `bad-signature` verdicts, which blocks the publication hold of epic polite-antivirus-bedbug. The comments of 2026-11-18 establish two classes, with UUID and content-hash binding intact on every entry:\n- Class A: 5,858 unique events (1,236 live + 4,622 SNAPSHOT-ledger) signed by the MCP box's op-cert keys (fingerprint prefixes 61689bf468f5, 6e2a8414bf53) under principal 594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d. Neither key is on 594c's keyring, so `verify_authorship_any_key` fails and `_verify_signed` returns `bad-signature`.\n- Class B: 906 unique ledger entries signed by keys that ARE on 594c's keyring, but compaction recorded `signer_pubkey: null`. `_verify_signed` (`src/rebar/_commands/verify_authorship.py`, the `if not is_live and ev.signer_pubkey is None: return BAD_SIGNATURE` short-circuit) rejects them before any verify.\n\nOperator decision (2026-11-19 comment): identity, authorization and op-cert certification are separate. A signature that is cryptographically valid, binds its event, and whose key is not on the claimed author's keyring is a separate identity finding, not `bad-signature`, and does not fail the enforced check. The output names the signing key. Class B is verified instead of rejected. The 874 unsigned events are out of scope. No keyring, trust-file, deployment, config-key, env-var or flag change.\n\n## Approach\nChange only the classifier and its report, in `src/rebar/_commands/verify_authorship.py`:\n1. Delete the null-`signer_pubkey` short-circuit in `_verify_signed`, so a null-signer ledger entry runs the same era verify and any-key verify as every other entry (class B then verifies).\n2. Add a sixth verdict `signer-not-on-keyring` (constant `SIGNER_NOT_ON_KEYRING`). When the any-key verify fails, `_verify_signed` runs one more verify through a new helper `_verifies_against_own_key(envelope, author_id)`: it reads the signer's public key out of the SSHSIG blob with the existing `sshsig.embedded_public_key` (`src/rebar/attest/sshsig.py`), builds a one-key trust root with `authorship.allowed_signers_from_keys`, and calls `registry.verify(authorship.AUTHORSHIP_KIND, ...)`, which is the same `ssh-keygen -Y verify` path (signature, namespace and principal all checked; never `check-novalidate`). Verified there means `signer-not-on-keyring`; otherwise `bad-signature`. The subject UUID/content-hash binding check already runs earlier in `_verify_signed` and still returns `bad-signature` on mismatch, so a non-binding or tampered signature keeps failing.\n3. `cli` counts the new verdict, prints it in the summary line after `key-not-valid-at-era`, lists it as a problem line, and does NOT add it to `enforced_not_verified`. Every listed signed problem line and every JSON report entry names the signing key as its OpenSSH `SHA256:` fingerprint, computed with the existing `signer_key_id` in `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_authorship.py` (null for unsigned or unparseable signatures). The JSON entry's `display` for the new verdict is `verified`, because the signature check passed; `verdict` carries the identity finding. The OK message states how many identity findings were reported but not enforced. Report-entry and message building move into small helpers so `cli` stays under its complexity ceiling.\n4. `src/rebar/schemas/verify_identity_report.schema.json` gains a required `signing_key` property (string or null) and documents the new verdict.\n5. `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_dry_run.py` parses the extended summary line (`_COUNT_RE` and `_COUNT_KEYS` gain `signer_not_on_keyring`).\n\nThis fits because the decision is a classification change inside one verifier: the embedded-key verify already exists for the same \"certification environment is not a gate\" reason (bug c21f docstring in `sshsig.embedded_public_key`), so no new trust source, config or mechanism is introduced. Riskiest assumption, already checked in-session: on the rewritten dry-run history, null-signer ledger entries for 4 distinct keys verify `certified` against their embedded key and bind their event, and the same envelope with a tampered payload returns `mismatch`.\n\nPortability: pure verification logic. It adds no subprocess, fd, path, encoding or permission handling; the extra verify reuses the existing `SshsigScheme.verify` call. No Windows sweep is needed.\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/_commands/verify_authorship.py (classifier, summary, report, helpers)\n- src/rebar/schemas/verify_identity_report.schema.json (signing_key, verdict docs)\n- src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_dry_run.py (summary-line parser)\n- tests/unit/test_identity_ts3_117b.py (replace the two Mallory \"forged\" tests, whose case is now the identity finding; add class-B and genuinely-bad cases)\n- tests/unit/test_identity_ac7_bff8.py (JSON entry carries signing_key)\n- tests/unit/test_reclaim_dry_run.py (parsed counts include the new key)\n- docs/identity.md, docs/identity-setup.md (verdict vocabulary)\n\n## Testing\nTests first (RED against origin/main), in `tests/unit/test_identity_ts3_117b.py`:\n- Identity finding, live: an event authored by identity Ada but signed by Mallory's key (well-formed Statement binding the event). Enforced `verify-identity --all` exits 0, prints `signer-not-on-keyring`, and prints Mallory's `SHA256:` fingerprint (from `ssh-keygen -lf mallory.pub`).\n- Identity finding, compacted: the same event after `rebar.compact` (ledger `signer_pubkey` is null) gives the same verdict and exit 0.\n- Class B: a properly signed event compacted, then its ledger entry's `signer_pubkey` set to null and committed; enforced run exits 0 with 0 bad-signature.\n- Genuinely bad signature: an envelope whose payload binds the event but whose signature bytes come from signing a different event's Statement with Mallory's key gives `bad-signature` and exit 1.\n- Non-binding: a Mallory-signed Statement for event X attached to event Y gives `bad-signature` and exit 1.\n- JSON: `--format json` on the identity-finding store emits an entry with `verdict` `signer-not-on-keyring`, `display` `verified`, `signing_key` equal to the fingerprint, validating against the schema.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A cryptographically valid, event-binding signature by a key not on the author's keyring is classified `signer-not-on-keyring`, names its `SHA256:` signing key, and does not fail the enforced check, for live and compacted events; verified by the identity-finding tests in `tests/unit/test_identity_ts3_117b.py`.\n- [ ] A null-`signer_pubkey` ledger entry signed by a keyring key classifies `verified`; verified by the class-B test.\n- [ ] A signature that fails cryptographic verification, or does not bind its event, still classifies `bad-signature` and exits 1; verified by the two bad-signature tests.\n- [ ] The JSON report entry carries `signing_key` and validates against `verify_identity_report.schema.json`; verified by `tests/unit/test_identity_ac7_bff8.py::test_json_report_shape_and_schema` and the new JSON test.\n- [ ] `reclaim_dry_run` parses the extended summary line; verified by `tests/unit/test_reclaim_dry_run.py::test_dry_run_writes_a_passing_evidence_packet` asserting the `signer_not_on_keyring` count.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes in the worktree, and the change merges through Gerrit with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Enforced `verify-identity --all` run with the merged build on the rewritten dry-run tickets history and on the original history reports 0 bad-signature, 0 unknown-author and 0 key-not-valid-at-era, with every remaining enforced non-verified event `unsigned`; the per-verdict counts for both histories are recorded as a comment on this ticket and on polite-antivirus-bedbug.\n  provenance: environment=operator Mac, local no-remote copies of the tickets history from the reclaim dry run; principal=agent session 3d705fec; privilege_posture=read-only verification, nothing pushed or published; instrument=`python -m rebar verify-identity --all` from a worktree at the merged origin/main — the histories are local data outside the repository, so the counts are attested in ticket comments.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795110108133269470,"uuid":"0b044d87-a407-4ea9-ab5e-8d26ab278aa2"}